          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Midnight commonly signifies the climactic dark hour of sudden divine visitation—judgment, deliverance, or Christ’s unexpected coming—while some occurrences remain literal or emphasize prayer and watchfulness.\",\"book_summary\":\"Midnight is the appointed midpoint of the night when judgment and liberation occur together, and it is additionally identified as an astronomically announced hour because the full moon reaches its height then.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"The dark or climactic hour of sudden divine intervention, especially judgment, deliverance, or eschatological arrival.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"The appointed hour of visitation producing a single reversal: judgment on the oppressor and release of the captive.\",\"core_relation\":\"BOOK_NARROWS\",\"extension_relation\":\"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book narrows the broad visitation motif to a coordinated judgment-and-liberation event and reclassifies prayer, praise, narrative, and bridegroom passages under that fixed pattern. It also adds a full-moon astronomical condition absent from the consensus.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"The shared identification of midnight with sudden divine visitation is not contradicted. However, the book’s claim of a fixed appointed meaning and intrinsic lunar announcement conflicts with the common view that midnight has no single fixed symbolism and no established connection to the moon.\",\"rationale\":\"The book retains the consensus’s central referent—sudden divine intervention at midnight—but gives it materially tighter boundaries and a specific reversal structure. Its lunar explanation is a novel supporting specification rather than a replacement for the central visitation referent, so the relationship is refined rather than divergent or wholly novel.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"The printed texts strongly attest midnight judgment, deliverance, prayer, and the bridegroom’s cry, especially in Exodus and Acts, but they do not themselves establish that every occurrence denotes one appointed judgment-liberation pattern or that midnight is uniquely announced by the full moon. Several references can function as ordinary time markers, and no cited text explicitly mentions the moon.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
      ],
